I found one critical premium-entitlement vulnerability, several high-risk authorization/data-retention issues, and a major payment integration gap.
Critical Findings
1.	Unauthenticated webhook can grant premium access
o	index.ts:188-280 accepts RevenueCat- and Stripe-shaped JSON without verifying a RevenueCat authorization header or Stripe signature.
o	An attacker can submit an event containing another user’s Firebase UID and set subscriptionStatus: 'premium'.
o	The existing regression test explicitly accepts an unsigned webhook payload in functions-regression.ts:99-118.
o	Impact: premium bypass, unauthorized entitlement changes, UID/account probing.
o	Fix: require and verify provider signatures before parsing or writing anything. Reject unsigned requests with 401.
2.	Payment API is not present in this repository
o	The client calls /api/subscription/checkout, /portal, /status, /cancel, and /resume from subscription-service.ts:130-180.
o	The checked-in backend contains only Firebase Functions and no implementation of those routes.
o	The deployed external API is therefore an unreviewed trust boundary. The existing AUDIT_REPORT.md:280-310 describes a Hono backend that is not currently present.
o	Action: obtain and audit the actual deployed payment API before production use.
3.	Premium access is enforced primarily on the client
o	useAuth.ts:90-115 derives premium access from the cached Firestore profile.
o	usePremiumUsage.ts:55-125 performs local limits and gates.
o	Public content collections are readable by everyone in firestore.rules:24-42.
o	Anyone can retrieve premium catalogue metadata, and client-side gates can be bypassed by modifying the app, intercepting requests, or replaying cached state.
o	Fix: enforce every paid capability server-side. Never rely on isPremium, local timers, AsyncStorage, or UI gates as authorization.
4.	Stale premium state survives failed verification
o	useAuth.ts:425-455 catches subscription refresh failures and retains the cached entitlement.
o	There is no effective check that lastVerifiedAt or subscriptionEndsAt is still valid before granting premium access.
o	Impact: revoked, expired, refunded, or charged-back users may retain access indefinitely while offline or while the API is unavailable.
o	Fix: implement bounded offline grace with an enforced expiry, then fail closed.
High-Risk Findings
5.	Account deletion is incomplete and reports false success
o	index.ts:138-181 deletes only selected documents, catches deletion failures, then deletes the Firebase Auth user and returns success.
o	It does not clearly delete userUsage, userFavorites, reminders under the nested path, audit records, AsyncStorage data, or Stripe customer/subscription data.
o	The Firestore paths are defined in firestore.rules:77-111.
o	Impact: GDPR/UK GDPR erasure failures, retained personal data, continued billing, inconsistent account state.
o	Fix: use an idempotent deletion job, enumerate every user-scoped collection, cancel/refund subscriptions according to policy, delete local data, and return success only after verified completion.
6.	Admin bootstrap depends on a shared secret
o	setup.tsx:68-115 asks users to enter ADMIN_SECRET_KEY and sends it to a user-supplied backend URL.
o	The repository does not contain the referenced /admin/setup implementation, but the existing audit documentation says it grants admin claims.
o	Impact: anyone who obtains the shared key can grant admin access to arbitrary accounts. Users can also accidentally submit the key to an attacker-controlled URL.
o	Fix: remove bootstrap from production, or use a one-time, server-generated setup flow restricted by deployment identity, allowlisted origin, and an already authenticated owner.
7.	Admin claim endpoint is overly powerful
o	index.ts:66-126 accepts arbitrary custom claims and merges them into a target user.
o	Impact: any compromised admin can grant arbitrary privileges, not just admin: true.
o	Fix: allow only an explicit schema of supported roles/permissions, prohibit changing the last super-admin, and require step-up authentication for role changes.
8.	Admin user management conflicts with Firestore rules
o	app/admin/(dashboard)/users.tsx/users.tsx#L40-L75) directly reads the entire users collection.
o	firestore.rules:47-65 permits only the document owner to read their profile, not an admin.
o	This currently appears to fail closed rather than leak data, but it indicates authorization logic is split and inconsistent.
o	Fix: route admin user queries through a server endpoint that verifies the admin claim and returns only the minimum fields required.
9.	User profile updates are too broad
o	useAuth.ts:396-423 accepts Partial<UserProfile> and serializes the complete profile.
o	Firestore rules protect subscription fields, but do not strictly restrict fields such as uid, email, usage statistics, or timestamps.
o	Users can tamper with their own analytics, streaks, and usage records.
o	Fix: use allowlisted update schemas and move all authoritative usage/account fields to trusted server writes.
Financial-data Protection
The client does not appear to process raw card numbers, which is good. Stripe secrets are also kept server-side in stripe-server.ts:20-57.
However:
•	The actual payment API is missing from the repository.
•	Its portal-session ownership checks cannot be verified.
•	Its webhook authenticity cannot be verified.
•	Stripe customer IDs are stored in user profiles and exposed to the owning user; these are not card numbers, but they are still personal/payment identifiers.
•	Do not return payment methods, invoices, billing addresses, or raw Stripe objects to the mobile client.
•	Billing portal URLs must always be created for the authenticated user’s server-resolved Stripe customer, never from a client-supplied customer ID.
Privacy and Legal Readiness
The app is not currently demonstrably ready for GDPR, UK GDPR, EU ePrivacy, CCPA/CPRA, US state privacy laws, PIPEDA, or Quebec Law 25.
Missing or unclear controls include:
•	Public, complete privacy notice and data-processing disclosures.
•	Controller identity, DPO/contact details, lawful bases, purposes, retention periods, and subprocessors.
•	Consent handling for optional analytics, notifications, tracking, and local storage.
•	Access, correction, portability, objection, restriction, and deletion workflows.
•	Verified deletion across Firebase, Stripe/RevenueCat, logs, backups, and local storage.
•	Data Processing Agreements and international transfer mechanisms for Firebase, Stripe, RevenueCat, hosting, and analytics.
•	A retention/deletion policy for audit logs and usage events.
•	Age/minor handling and COPPA/UK Children’s Code assessment.
•	Health-data assessment: the app makes claims relating to sleep, healing, pain, mental health, DNA, trauma, and medical conditions. These can create health-related inferences and increase GDPR/UK GDPR sensitivity.
•	US health claims should be reviewed for FTC advertising substantiation and consumer-protection risk. HIPAA likely does not automatically apply, but that depends on the business model and partners.
•	EU/UK/Canada consumer requirements for free trials, automatic renewal, cancellation, pricing, VAT/taxes, and withdrawal rights.
The in-app “Privacy Policy” text in app/(tabs)/settings.tsx/settings.tsx#L560-L590) is not a substitute for a legally complete, publicly accessible privacy notice and currently makes broad claims that require verification.
Required Remediation Order
1.	Disable the subscription webhook and payment production flow until signature verification is implemented.
2.	Audit and secure the external /api/subscription/* service.
3.	Move premium authorization to trusted server-side checks.
4.	Remove or redesign shared-secret admin bootstrap.
5.	Implement complete, verified account deletion and subscription cancellation.
6.	Tighten Firestore update schemas and admin access paths.
7.	Publish privacy/terms documents and implement rights, retention, consent, and subprocessor controls.
8.	Complete Google Play/App Store billing compliance review. Stripe Checkout for digital subscriptions is generally not acceptable on Google Play or iOS where platform billing is required.
This was a static repository audit. I did not have access to the deployed payment API, Firebase production project, Stripe dashboard, RevenueCat configuration, cloud IAM, logs, or production Firestore, so those require a separate authenticated penetration and configuration review.
